    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0454" n="358" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg358"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.6176/84</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sterling</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-04-07"><hi rendition="#italic">April 7,
                        1926</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received April 20.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 918</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Embassy’s telegram No. 372
                    dated December 4, 11 a.m., 1925,<anchor n="97" xml:id="d185-f1"/> in which the
                    Ambassador gave the substance of his interview with Sir Austen Chamberlain
                    concerning restrictions in the export of crude rubber and other raw materials,
                    and when the Ambassador left with him a memorandum on the position of the United
                    States Government,<anchor n="98" xml:id="d185-f2"/> I have the honor to enclose
                    a copy, in triplicate, of a note just received from the Foreign Office dated
                    April 6, 1926, in reply to the memorandum.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">F. A. Sterling</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d185-a1.xml" xpointer="d185-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
